---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SEOHead from '../components/SEOHead.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <SEOHead
    slot="head"
    title="Architecture | Jacob Steelsmith"
    description="Technical architecture documentation for the resume site, including infrastructure diagrams, CI/CD pipeline, and design decisions with rationale."
  />

  <h1>Site Architecture</h1>
  <p class="page-intro">
    This page documents how this resume site is built, deployed, and operated. It demonstrates a hybrid
    Infrastructure-as-Code approach using CloudFormation for AWS resources and Terraform for cross-platform configuration.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
    <h2 id="overview-heading">System Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The site is a static Astro application hosted on AWS with a RAG-powered AI chatbot. Infrastructure is fully
      codified across two IaC tools, deployed via GitHub Actions with OIDC authentication — no long-lived credentials stored anywhere.
    </p>

    <div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="High-level architecture diagram showing the flow from developer to visitor">
      <pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        GitHub Repository                            │
│  src/ ─ Astro pages    infrastructure/ ─ CloudFormation + Terraform │
│  knowledge-base/ ─ RAG content    .github/workflows/ ─ CI/CD       │
└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┘
                               │ push to main
                               ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      GitHub Actions CI/CD                           │
│  Build Astro → Validate CFN → OIDC Auth → S3 Sync → CF Invalidate │
└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┘
                               │ OIDC assume role
                               ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     AWS (us-east-1)                                 │
│                                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────┐               │
│  │ Route 53 │───▶│  CloudFront    │───▶│ S3 Bucket│               │
│  │ DNS      │    │  (OAC, HTTPS)  │    │ (static) │               │
│  └──────────┘    └────────────────┘    └──────────┘               │
│                                                                     │
│  ┌──────────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌──────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ API Gateway  │─▶│ Lambda │─▶│ Bedrock Knowledge Base   │       │
│  │ + WAF        │  │ (RAG)  │  │ (Titan Embeddings + Claude)│      │
│  └──────────────┘  └────────┘  └──────────────────────────┘       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                               ▲
                               │ HTTPS
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      Visitor Browser                                │
│  Static pages served via CloudFront CDN                            │
│  Chat widget communicates with API Gateway                         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="hosting-heading">
    <h2 id="hosting-heading">Hosting Infrastructure (CloudFormation)</h2>
    <p>
      A single CloudFormation stack in <code>infrastructure/template.yaml</code> provisions all AWS hosting resources.
      The stack is deployed in us-east-1 (required for ACM certificates used with CloudFront).
    </p>

    <h3>Resources</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>S3 Bucket</strong> — Static asset storage with all public access blocked</li>
      <li><strong>CloudFront Distribution</strong> — CDN with HTTP/2 + HTTP/3, Brotli/gzip compression, HTTPS redirect, TLS 1.2 minimum</li>
      <li><strong>Origin Access Control (OAC)</strong> — Restricts S3 reads to CloudFront only</li>
      <li><strong>ACM Certificate</strong> — TLS certificate with DNS validation via Route 53</li>
      <li><strong>Route 53 Records</strong> — A and AAAA alias records pointing to CloudFront</li>
      <li><strong>Response Headers Policy</strong> — HSTS (1 year), X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options DENY, CSP</li>
      <li><strong>API Gateway (REST)</strong> — POST /chat endpoint for the RAG chatbot</li>
      <li><strong>AWS WAF</strong> — Per-IP rate limiting (10 req/min) on the chat API</li>
      <li><strong>Lambda Function</strong> — RAG agent handler with least-privilege Bedrock access</li>
      <li><strong>Bedrock Knowledge Base</strong> — Vector store with Titan Text Embeddings V2 (1024 dimensions)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="terraform-heading">
    <h2 id="terraform-heading">Cross-Platform Configuration (Terraform)</h2>
    <p>
      Terraform manages resources that CloudFormation cannot — GitHub repository settings and the AWS OIDC provider
      for GitHub Actions. Configuration lives in <code>infrastructure/terraform/</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Resources</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>IAM OIDC Provider</strong> — Enables GitHub Actions to assume AWS roles via <code>unfunco/oidc-github/aws</code> module</li>
      <li><strong>IAM Deploy Role</strong> — Scoped to S3 put/delete/list and CloudFront invalidation only</li>
      <li><strong>Branch Protection</strong> — Main branch requires 1 approval + passing CI checks</li>
      <li><strong>Actions Secrets/Variables</strong> — AWS_ACCOUNT_ID, OIDC_ROLE_ARN, S3_BUCKET_NAME, CLOUDFRONT_DIST_ID</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      State is stored in S3 with DynamoDB locking for safe concurrent access.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cicd-heading">
    <h2 id="cicd-heading">CI/CD Pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      GitHub Actions handles build, validation, and deployment with zero stored credentials.
    </p>

    <div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="CI/CD pipeline flow diagram">
      <pre><code>Pull Request:
  ┌──────────┐    ┌───────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐
  │ Checkout │───▶│ Build     │───▶│ Validate CFN        │
  │          │    │ Astro     │    │ Template            │
  └──────────┘    └───────────┘    └─────────────────────┘

Push to main:
  ┌──────────┐    ┌───────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌───────────┐    ┌────────────┐
  │ Checkout │───▶│ Build     │───▶│ OIDC     │───▶│ S3 Sync   │───▶│ CloudFront │
  │          │    │ Astro     │    │ Auth     │    │ dist/     │    │ Invalidate │
  └──────────┘    └───────────┘    └──────────┘    └───────────┘    └────────────┘</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OIDC Authentication</strong> — GitHub Actions assumes an IAM role using short-lived tokens, no access keys</li>
      <li><strong>Pinned Actions</strong> — All GitHub Actions use exact version pins for reproducible builds</li>
      <li><strong>CloudFormation Validation</strong> — Template is validated on every PR before merge</li>
      <li><strong>Automatic Invalidation</strong> — CloudFront cache is cleared on every deploy</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="chatbot-heading">
    <h2 id="chatbot-heading">RAG Chatbot</h2>
    <p>
      The site includes an AI-powered chat widget that answers questions about skills, experience, and projects
      using Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Amazon Bedrock.
    </p>

    <div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="RAG chatbot data flow diagram">
      <pre><code>Visitor Question
       │
       ▼
┌──────────────┐     ┌────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────┐
│ API Gateway  │────▶│ Lambda (RAG)   │────▶│ Bedrock Knowledge   │
│ + WAF Rate   │     │                │     │ Base                │
│   Limiting   │     │ 1. Validate    │     │                     │
└──────────────┘     │ 2. Retrieve    │     │ Titan Embeddings V2 │
                     │ 3. Generate    │     │ + Claude 3 Haiku    │
                     │ 4. Filter      │     │ + Vector Store      │
                     └────────────────┘     └─────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                     ┌────────────────┐
                     │ Response with  │
                     │ sources &amp;      │
                     │ attributions   │
                     └────────────────┘</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Knowledge Base Pipeline</h3>
    <p>
      Content from <code>knowledge-base/</code> (skills, experience, projects, certifications, code samples) is
      chunked into 500–1000 token segments with 10% overlap, embedded via Titan Text Embeddings V2, and stored
      in a Bedrock-managed OpenSearch Serverless vector store.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="decisions-heading">
    <h2 id="decisions-heading">Design Decisions</h2>

    <div class="decision-grid">
      <article class="decision-card">
        <h3>CloudFormation vs Terraform for AWS Resources</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>Decision</dt>
          <dd>Use CloudFormation for all AWS hosting infrastructure</dd>
          <dt>Alternatives Considered</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Terraform for everything</strong> — More portable, but adds state management complexity for AWS-native resources that CloudFormation handles natively</li>
              <li><strong>AWS CDK</strong> — Higher-level abstractions, but adds a compilation step and obscures the actual resource definitions</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>Rationale</dt>
          <dd>
            CloudFormation provides native AWS integration with automatic rollback, drift detection, and no external state to manage.
            Terraform is used only where CloudFormation cannot reach (GitHub configuration, OIDC provider module). This hybrid approach
            demonstrates proficiency with both tools while using each where it excels.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <h3>Bedrock Managed Vector Store vs Self-Managed OpenSearch</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>Decision</dt>
          <dd>Use Amazon Bedrock's auto-provisioned OpenSearch Serverless vector store</dd>
          <dt>Alternatives Considered</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Self-managed OpenSearch cluster</strong> — Full control over indexing and queries, but requires capacity planning, patching, and monitoring</li>
              <li><strong>Pinecone or other third-party vector DB</strong> — Purpose-built for vectors, but adds external dependency and egress costs</li>
              <li><strong>pgvector on RDS</strong> — Familiar SQL interface, but overkill for a single-purpose knowledge base</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>Rationale</dt>
          <dd>
            Bedrock manages the entire vector store lifecycle — provisioning, scaling, indexing, and backups. For a personal
            resume site with a small, infrequently-updated knowledge base, the operational simplicity outweighs the reduced
            control. No infrastructure to maintain means lower cost and less operational burden.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <h3>OIDC vs Long-Lived Credentials for CI/CD</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>Decision</dt>
          <dd>Use GitHub Actions OIDC to assume an IAM role with short-lived credentials</dd>
          <dt>Alternatives Considered</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>IAM user access keys stored as GitHub secrets</strong> — Simpler setup, but credentials never expire and must be manually rotated</li>
              <li><strong>AWS SSO / Identity Center</strong> — Enterprise-grade, but overly complex for a single-repo deployment</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>Rationale</dt>
          <dd>
            OIDC tokens are scoped to a specific repository and branch, expire automatically, and leave no permanent credentials
            to leak or rotate. The IAM role's trust policy ensures only the correct repository can assume it. This is the
            AWS-recommended approach for GitHub Actions and eliminates an entire class of credential management risks.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <h3>REST API Gateway vs HTTP API Gateway</h3>
        <dl>
          <dt>Decision</dt>
          <dd>Use REST API Gateway (v1) for the chat endpoint</dd>
          <dt>Alternatives Considered</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>HTTP API Gateway (v2)</strong> — Lower cost and latency, but lacks native usage plans and API key support</li>
              <li><strong>Application Load Balancer</strong> — Supports WebSocket for streaming, but adds always-on cost and complexity</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>Rationale</dt>
          <dd>
            REST API Gateway provides built-in usage plans for global rate limiting (100 req/min), request validation,
            and straightforward WAF integration for per-IP rate limiting. The slightly higher per-request cost is negligible
            at the expected traffic volume, and the operational features justify the choice over HTTP API.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="static-site-heading">
    <h2 id="static-site-heading">Static Site Generation</h2>
    <p>
      The frontend is built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> configured for fully static output.
      No server-side runtime is required — all pages are pre-rendered HTML served directly from S3 via CloudFront.
    </p>

    <h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Zero JavaScript by default — only the chat widget and mobile nav add client-side JS</li>
      <li>Semantic HTML with proper heading hierarchy and ARIA landmarks</li>
      <li>Responsive layout from 320px to 2560px without horizontal scrolling</li>
      <li>SEO metadata (Open Graph, canonical URLs, sitemap) on every page</li>
      <li>Security headers enforced at the CDN edge (HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .diagram {
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    background-color: var(--color-bg-alt);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-md);
  }

  .diagram pre {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .diagram code {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', 'Fira Code', monospace;
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  h3 {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .decision-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .decision-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .decision-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    background-color: var(--color-bg);
  }

  .decision-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .decision-card dl {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .decision-card dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .decision-card dt:first-of-type {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .decision-card dd {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .decision-card dd ul {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
